  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Significant precipitation is expected Tuesday \nand Tuesday night, with light wintry precipitation possible over\nMoosehead and North Woods areas early Tuesday. The wintry \nprecipitation is not expected to impact travel, and flooding is \nnot expected.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nLow pressure across the Great Lakes will draw a warm front\ntoward the region tonight through early tomorrow. Overrunning \nprecipitation in advance of the warm front will begin to develop\nacross the region later tonight then expand across the region \nTuesday. Precipitation will generally be in the form of rain. \nHowever, dependent on the onset time, just enough cold air could\nexist across northern areas and the west-central highlands to \nalso support an interval of light snow or sleet early Tuesday \nmorning. Little, or no, snow or sleet accumulation is expected \nexcept possibly across higher elevation areas of Moosehead and\nNorth Woods. To the east dry air in the boundary layer is going\nto slow the onset of precipitation with a ton of Virga occurring.\nThe low approaches Tuesday, then crosses the region Tuesday \nnight with rain which could persist into early Wednesday. Rain \ntotals Tuesday into Wednesday will max out generally 0.75-1.25 \ninches for much of the CWA with only 0.4-0.6 inch across the St.\nJohn Valley.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Widespread rain is likely again Friday/Friday \nnight. Chance of accumulating wintry precipitation for northern \nareas. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nAn active weather pattern will remain, with another low pressure\nsystem passing through the area from west to east around \nFriday afternoon into Saturday. This is still a lot of \nuncertainty, but it does appear very likely most places will get\nat least 0.5 inch of precipitation, with some places exceeding \n0.5 inch. Gusty winds and wintry precipitation are both \npossibilities depending on the track of the system, with chances\nof both increasing this forecast cycle. Wind gusts of at least \n30 mph are possible with increasing possibility of higher gusts.\nAccumulating snowfall chances are increasing, especially for \nthe North Woods, Moosehead and Western St. John Valley areas\nalong with the higher terrain of Route 11. Ensembles and \ndeterministic guidance are strongly signaling the chances of \nwintry precipitation in the area but timing is all over the \nplace. Timing will be important as nighttime has the highest \npotential of wintry precip.",
